  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "WIRED investigates where Flock Safety assembles its license plate reader cameras, finding the company has quietly stopped claiming its devices are made in the USA and increasingly imports components from Primax Electronics in Thailand. The article traces Flock's manufacturing history from Atlanta assembly to contract manufacturers East West and Plexus, raises supply-chain security questions, and contrasts with Flock's US-made drone facility in Smyrna, Georgia.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "For years, Flock touted publicly that its devices were produced in the United States. But more recent marketing materials from the company reviewed by WIRED no longer specify where its cameras come from.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "Import records collected by ImportGenius show the company has been increasingly bringing \"commodity\" cameras, radar systems, and other components over from Primax Electronics in Thailand.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    },
    {
      "quote": "All products that Flock builds are National Defense Authorization Act compliant",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    },
    {
      "quote": "If any part of the supply chain is compromised, we would never know. That's a much bigger problem than where the final product is assembled.",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    },
    {
      "quote": "Domestic manufacturing is no substitute for accountability over how these systems are built, sold, and used",
      "paragraph_idx": 39
    }
  ],
